# Analytics fixture design

## Caller usage

Keep the shared backend fixture analytics-free. Opt in only where a test needs the browser SDK:

```ts
// tests/search-tracking.spec.ts
import { expect, test } from "./backend";

test.use({ posthog: true });
```

The entity-details same-origin traffic test imports the same `test` and does not opt in. It receives a fresh app worker without PostHog environment values, so browser requests continue to exercise the app's same-origin path.

## Fixture shape

```ts
type Fixtures = { backend: Backend };
type WorkerFixtures = {
  posthog: boolean; // Worker startup configuration; default false.
  application: { url: string; backend: Backend };
};

export const test = base.extend<Fixtures & { posthog: boolean }, WorkerFixtures>({
  posthog: [false, { option: true, scope: "worker" }],
  application: [async ({ posthog }, use, workerInfo) => {
    const env = {
      ...process.env,
      ...(posthog
        ? {
            PUBLIC_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN: "phc_test_search_tracking",
            PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST: "https://posthog.test",
          }
        : {}),
      PORT: String(4300 + workerInfo.workerIndex),
      // HOST and EXPOSED_API_ORIGIN remain as today.
    };
    // Spawn, readiness polling, use, and cleanup remain as today.
  }, { scope: "worker" }],
  // Existing page, baseURL, and backend fixtures remain unchanged.
});
```

`posthog` is a Playwright worker option, so its value is part of worker identity. Tests with `posthog: true` get an app process started with the fake analytics config; tests with the default `false` get an app process without it. Each worker has its own process and backend as it does now.

## Why this shape

This makes app startup configuration explicit at the test call site and keeps the worker fixture deterministic. The option fixture is the smallest addition: one typed boolean, one conditional environment fragment, and one opt-in in the tracking spec. A separate fixture module would split shared application setup or require another test export while adding little isolation. Mutating `process.env` is weaker: it changes runner-wide state and can race with parallel worker startup, even if code tries to restore the old values. Passing the option into the worker and constructing that child's environment avoids both problems.
